# Stable Integration Baseline (SIB)

CodeSleuth release construction follows this invariant:

`SIB -> integration build -> feature composition -> acceptance -> RC -> release`

The `SIB` branch is the known-good starting point for a new release. At policy introduction it points to `c5e41a73b84e65645dec5d0a4032b19928291193`.

## Required behavior

- Do not implement release-bound feature work directly on `SIB`.
- Start new release/integration work from `SIB` or from an explicitly accepted descendant.
- Apply changes incrementally. After each substantial layer, run the repository's full canonical acceptance gate.
- A feature being green on an old branch or old base is not acceptance evidence for the current composition.
- Only an exact tested commit may be treated as an accepted integration state.
- Preserve current semantics when transplanting stale work. Re-apply the intended delta rather than replacing newer files wholesale.
- Never weaken, skip, xfail, or rewrite acceptance tests to promote a change.
- Record the tested commit SHA and gate result when claiming compatibility.
- RC status is downstream of completed, accepted feature composition. SIB itself is not an RC.
- Repointing `SIB` is a separate maintainer promotion decision after a proven release/post-release state exists. Never advance it as a side effect of ordinary feature work.

See [`CONTRIBUTING.md`](../../CONTRIBUTING.md) for the contributor-facing contract.
